"Design development (2026-08-09, with user): TS assets stop being arrays referenced by index. Authoring becomes Record<name, asset> (the key IS the name; asset bodies carry no name field; duplicate keys are a TS compile error, enforcing per-kind uniqueness at authoring time; keyof typeof gives literal name types for cross-references). Anonymous instantiate-blob lists stay arrays. KEYING DECISION (recommended, pending user confirm): asset tables keep integer ids as PK/runtime key; name is a separate unique column = canonical identity. Rationale: (1) hot per-entity component rows (action_state per tick, trait_ids/action_ids vecs, baseline refs) dominate subscription wire volume -- string keys would bloat every entity row and every tick update vs 4-byte ints; (2) asset tables are tiny, so name lookup speed there is irrelevant, but string PKs would put string compares in every hot reference lookup; (3) name-as-PK recreates the migration problem: a rename would rewrite every referencing row, whereas name-for-identity + id-for-reference makes renames touch one row and index-moves impossible by construction (server preserves ids by name-matching across pushes). CONVERSION ASYMMETRY (user decision): forward name->id ONLY on server at push/migration time (server interns: match names to existing rows, keep their ids, assign fresh ids to new names, resolve all name cross-refs in payload to ids before insert -- migration authority must live where the data lives). Backward id->name ONLY on client, from subscribed asset tables carrying (id, name), for display/debug. Each site gets a comment explaining why the direction differs."